 <h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Supplementary methods</h2>
-<h3 id="odor-complete-curves-method">Complete odor curves with a fixed reference denominator</h3>
+<details class="supplementary-data__group"><summary id="odor-complete-curves-method">Complete odor curves with a fixed reference denominator</summary><div class="supplementary-data__body">
 <p>For stimulus s and construct c, subtract the measured 48 s F0 endpoint change from every F1 point, then subtract DMSO at the same local acquisition time. Divide by the fixed VUAA1-minus-DMSO endpoint difference at 168 s. The final point equals Results Figure 14; VUAA1 equals 1 at that endpoint by definition. F0 is a constant correction, not an extrapolated trace; initial offsets remain. Test 1 retains its four raw stages.</p>
 <p class="measurement-equation">Ss,c(t) = 100 × [F1s,c(t)/F1s,c(0) − 1] − δF0s,c;  Ns,c(t) = [Ss,c(t) − SDMSO,c(t)] / [SVUAA1,c(168) − SDMSO,c(168)]</p>
-<h3 id="control-statistics">Nominal object-count uncertainty</h3>
+</div></details><details class="supplementary-data__group"><summary id="control-statistics">Nominal object-count uncertainty</summary><div class="supplementary-data__body">
 <p>Fisher tests use [[positive, eligible − positive], [blank-positive, blank-eligible − blank-positive]] with the greater alternative. Holm adjustment orders p-values, multiplies by the remaining test count, applies the cumulative maximum and caps at 1. Wilson intervals use z = 1.96 and the eligible object count.</p>
